First off, the coins are identical, which means that it doesn't matter which specific coin goes into which box; all that matters is how many coins end up in each box. On the other hand, the boxes are different, so putting 3 coins in Box A and 2 coins in Box B is considered a different arrangement than putting 3 coins in Box B and 2 coins in Box A. That distinction is important because if the boxes were identical, we might have to worry about overcounting or undercounting symmetries, but since they're different, each distribution is unique based on which box has which number of coins. 

